✅ What Are Date-Time APIs in Java?
Date-Time APIs in Java are classes and methods provided by the Java language to handle:

Dates (like 2025-07-06)

Times (like 14:30:00)

Date-times (like 2025-07-06T14:30:00)

Time zones

Formatting and parsing

Arithmetic (add/subtract days, hours, etc.)

🔹 Two Main Date-Time APIs in Java
| API                                    | Description                                      |
| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| `java.util.Date`, `java.util.Calendar` | **Old**, error-prone API (pre-Java 8) // before date-time api it contain local time and when the application deployed other time zone the date time error occur
| `java.time.*` (Java 8+)                | **Modern**, powerful, thread-safe, and preferred |


🔸 1. Old API (Before Java 8)
java.util.Date

java.util.Calendar

java.text.SimpleDateFormat

❌ These are mutable and not thread-safe, and are not recommended for new applications.

🔸 2. New API: java.time Package (Java 8+)
Introduced in Java 8 as part of JSR-310, inspired by Joda-Time, and now the standard.

✅ Key Classes:
| Class               | Description                                            | Example                                     |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------- |
| `LocalDate`         | Date only (no time)                                    | `2025-07-15`                                |
| `LocalTime`         | Time only (no date)                                    | `14:30:00`                                  |
| `LocalDateTime`     | Date + Time (no time zone)                             | `2025-07-15T14:30`                          |
| `ZonedDateTime`     | Date + Time + TimeZone                                 | `2025-07-15T14:30+05:00[Asia/Karachi]`      |
| `Instant`           | Timestamp (machine time in UTC)                        | `2025-07-15T09:30:00Z`                      |
| `Period`            | Amount of time in **dates** (e.g., 5 days, 2 months)   | `P5D`                                       |
| `Duration`          | Amount of time in **time** (e.g., 3 hours, 10 minutes) | `PT3H`                                      |
| `DateTimeFormatter` | For formatting and parsing dates                       | `DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MM-yyyy")` |

✅ Advantages of the New API
| Feature                | Benefit                                          |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| **Immutable**          | Thread-safe and bug-free                         |
| **Fluent API**         | Easy to read and chain methods                   |
| **Time zone support**  | Precise handling with `ZonedDateTime`            |
| **Clear separation**   | Different classes for date, time, and timestamps |
| **ISO 8601 compliant** | Better interoperability with APIs                |

🧪 Examples
✅ LocalDate (just date)

LocalDate today = LocalDate.now();
LocalDate birthday = LocalDate.of(1995, 5, 23);
✅ LocalTime (just time)

LocalTime now = LocalTime.now();
✅ LocalDateTime (date + time)

LocalDateTime current = LocalDateTime.now();
✅ ZonedDateTime (date + time + time zone)

ZonedDateTime zdt = ZonedDateTime.now(ZoneId.of("Asia/Karachi"));
✅ Formatting Date

DateTimeFormatter formatter = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MM-yyyy");
String formatted = LocalDate.now().format(formatter);

🆚 Why Prefer java.time API?
| Old API                | New API                                 |
| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| `Date`, `Calendar`     | `LocalDate`, `ZonedDateTime`, `Instant` |
| Mutable                | Immutable                               |
| Not thread-safe        | Thread-safe                             |
| Confusing design       | Clean and modern design                 |
| Poor time zone support | Excellent time zone support             |

